d as **\"In stock\"** with a customer rating of **77% (out of 100) based on 12 reviews** [1].\n\nAt $57.99, this speaker falls **under the $60 USD budget ceiling** by $2.01. However, this does not include any applicable sales tax or potential shipping costs, which could push the total above $60 depending on the buyer's location and shipping method.\n\nA related accessory — the **Khanka Hard Travel Case for Ortizan Portable Bluetooth Speaker, IPX7 Waterproof Wireless Speaker** (SKU B0922RJTMV, Case Only, Black) — is priced at **$12.99** with a rating of 93% from 12 reviews, and is available in five colors (Black, Blue, Gray, Pink, Teal) [2]. This would be an additional cost if needed.\n\n**Regarding the \"40W\" model designation:** The product title explicitly states \"40W Loud Stereo Sound,\" and this is the primary differentiator in the product line. Ortizan also sells a lower-tier model titled **\"Ortizan Portable Bluetooth Speaker, IPX7 Waterproof Wireless Speaker with 24W Loud Stereo Sound\"** (SKU B08V4PTCMR) priced at **$37.99** with a rating of 72% from 12 reviews, available in seven colors (Blue, Gray, Navy, Pink, Red, Sand, Teal) [3]. This confirms that Ortizan uses wattage as a model-tier differentiator, with \"40W\" representing the higher-tier product in their lineup. No specific model number (e.g., X10, X20, X30) was found in any search results — the speaker appears to be sold simply under its descriptive title with the \"40W\" designation as the key identifier.\n\n**No information on price fluctuations, coupons, or official Ortizan website pricing was found.** The official Ortizan website (ortizan.com) did not appear in any search results, nor did any dedicated product page on an Ortizan-branded domain.\n\n---\n\n### 2. OUTPUT POWER AND DISTORTION WORDING\n\n**Wattage Claims:**\n\nThe product title claims **\"40W Loud Stereo Sound\"** [1]. However, the search results do not include any detailed technical specifications breaking down:\n- Whether this 40W is **peak power or RMS (continuous/Root Mean Square) power**\n- The **number of drivers** and their **individual wattage ratings** (e.g., two 20W drivers, or a woofer + tweeter combination)\n- Whether the 40W is total system power across all drivers\n\nThe Ortizan 24W model uses identical language structure: \"24W Loud Stereo Sound\" [3], suggesting this is a consistent but vague marketing claim across the product line, not a detailed technical specification.\n\n**Distortion (THD) Specifications:**\n\n**No distortion (THD — Total Harmonic Distortion) specifications were found in any of the search results.** This includes:\n- No THD percentage at rated power\n- No THD+N (Total Harmonic Distortion + Noise) figure\n- No distortion measurement with an associated frequency range\n- No mention of distortion in any of the product listing summaries or review excerpts\n\nThis is a **critical gap and red flag.** A 40W claim without any accompanying distortion specification is, as the research brief notes, \"suspicious.\" In the audio industry, output power without distortion context is essentially meaningless — a speaker can output 40W with 10% THD or 40W with 0.1% THD, and the sound quality difference would be dramatic. Budget speaker brands commonly inflate wattage figures by quoting peak power at high distortion levels. Without published THD figures, there is **no way to verify the quality of the 40W output**, and the absence of this information should be considered a significant disclosure gap.\n\n---\n\n### 3. 360-DEGREE SOUND AND PASSIVE RADIATOR CLAIMS\n\nThe search results do not contain any explicit mention of **\"360-degree sound\"** or **\"passive radiator\"** in the Ortizan 40W speaker's product listings or descriptions.\n\nThe product title for both the 40W and 24W models emphasizes **\"Loud Stereo Sound\"** rather than 360-degree or omnidirectional sound dispersion [1][3]. This language — \"Stereo Sound\" rather than \"360 Sound\" — suggests a directional stereo presentation rather than an omnidirectional design.\n\nNo published measurements, polar response plots, or acoustic dispersion patterns were found for this speaker. No third-party reviews with measurement data appeared in any search results.\n\n**Regarding passive radiator design:** No information about passive radiators — including their size, number, placement, material, or implementation — was found in any of the search results. The product listings do not appear to market or mention passive radiator technology as a feature, which stands in contrast to competitors like the Soundcore Flare 2 that explicitly highlight their passive radiator and 360-degree sound design.\n\n---\n\n### 4. IPX7 WATERPROOF RATING\n\n**IPX7 Claim:**\n\nThe product title explicitly claims **\"IPX7 Waterproof\"** for this speaker [1].\n\n**What IPX7 Means (Standardized Testing):**\n- **IPX7** is defined by the IEC 60529 standard as protection against the effects of temporary immersion in water\n- Testing conditions: Immersion in **1 meter (approximately 3.3 feet) of fresh water for 30 minutes**, under static (non-moving) conditions\n- The \"X\" in IPX7 means there is **no dust protection rating** — only water ingress protection is certified\n- IPX7 does **not** mean the speaker is designed for continuous underwater use, saltwater exposure, or exposure to moving/pressurized water (that would be IPX8 or IPX6, respectively)\n\n**Ortizan's Official Claim:** The product title for the SKU B08KCX841R listing explicitly states \"IPX7 Waterproof\" [1], which means Ortizan is officially claiming compliance with the IPX7 standard. However, the search results do not provide access to the full product description, warranty terms, or any fine print about what IPX7 covers and what it excludes.\n\n**Owner Reports / Water Exposure Incidents:** **No owner reports specifically about this Ortizan model and water exposure incidents were found in any of the search results.** The search results did not include forum discussions, Reddit threads, or review excerpts addressing actual water exposure experiences with this specific Ortizan 40W model.\n\n---\n\n### 5. BATTERY CAVEATS\n\n**Stated Battery Life:** The product title claims **\"15H Playtime\"** (15 hours) and specifies a **\"6600 mAh\"** battery capacity [1].\n\n**Power Bank Function:** The title advertises a **\"Power Bank Function for Party\"** — meaning the speaker can be used to charge external devices (phones, tablets) from its 6600 mAh battery [1]. This is an additional battery drain factor.\n\n**Bluetooth Version:** The speaker uses **Bluetooth 5.0** [1].\n\n**Fine-Print Conditions (Not Found in Search Results):**\n- Volume level assumptions for the 15-hour claim (e.g., 50% volume, 70% volume, etc.)\n- Whether the 15-hour claim includes or excludes the lighting effects (if any)\n- Whether the lighting effects (if present) impact battery life and by how much\n- Charging time (how long to fully recharge the 6600 mAh battery)\n- Battery degradation notes or warranty coverage for battery capacity over time\n- Whether the power bank function reduces the 15-hour playtime figure\n\n**Comparison with lower-tier model:** The Ortizan 24W model (SKU B08V4PTCMR) claims **\"30H Playtime\"** with a **\"66ft Bluetooth Range\"** [3] — notably double the battery life of the 40W model, suggesting the higher wattage output of the 40W model comes at a significant battery life cost.\n\n---\n\n### 6. HI-RES-OVER-BLUETOOTH CLAIMS\n\n**Bluetooth Codecs Listed in Product Title/Specs:**\n\nThe product title mentions only **\"Bluetooth 5.0\"** [1] — no specific mention of hi-res audio codecs such as:\n- **LDAC** (Sony's hi-res codec, up to 990 kbps)\n- **aptX HD** (Qualcomm's 24-bit codec, 576 kbps)\n- **aptX Adaptive** (Qualcomm's variable-bitrate codec)\n- Any proprietary hi-res codec\n\n**Assessment:**\n\nThe absence of any hi-res codec claims in the product title or listing excerpts is notable. Bluetooth 5.0 is a connection standard, not an audio codec — it provides the hardware layer for Bluetooth communication but does not guarantee any particular audio quality codec. Without LDAC, aptX HD, or aptX Adaptive support, the speaker would rely on the **standard Bluetooth SBC (Subband Codec)** or possibly **AAC** for audio transmission. SBC is a lossy codec with limited bitrate, and AAC performance varies significantly between Android and iOS devices.\n\nAs a budget brand (the 40W model is priced at $57.99), it would be unusual for Ortizan to include premium codec licensing fees (Qualcomm charges license fees for aptX variants, Sony for LDAC) without prominently advertising those features. The fact that no hi-res codec is mentioned in the product title — which is typically used to highlight every competitive feature — strongly suggests that **this speaker does not support hi-res Bluetooth codecs** and relies on standard SBC/AAC transmission. Any implied \"hi-res\" capability would be **unverifiable and inflated** based on the available evidence.\n\nNo third-party testing of Bluetooth codec support, latency measurements, or signal quality analysis was found in any search results.\n\n---\n\n## List of All Relevant Sources (with citations in the report)\n\n**[1]** Ortizan Portable Bluetooth Speaker, 40W Loud Stereo Sound, IPX7 Waterproof Bluetooth Speakers with Bluetooth 5.0, Dual Pairing, 6600 mAh, 15H Playtime, Power Bank Function for Party — Amazon listing (SKU B08KCX841R)\n- URL: `http://localhost:7770/ortizan-portable-bluetooth-speaker-40w-loud-stereo-sound-ipx7-waterproof-bluetooth-speakers-with-bluetooth-5-0-dual-pairing-6600-mah-15h-playtime-power-bank-function-for-party.html`\n- **Used for:** Price ($57.99), stock status, rating (77%/12 reviews), SKU (B08KCX841R), product title and all named features (40W, IPX7, Bluetooth 5.0, dual pairing, 6600 mAh, 15H playtime, power bank function), confirming \"40W\" is the key model differentiator, confirming IPX7 claim, confirming battery specs, conf